preview2-shim (jco): an accepted TCP socket gets its own poll state.

Not applied in the tests. socketTcpAccept gives the accepted socket the
input stream's poll state, so a tcp-socket.subscribe() pollable alive
when wasi-libc drops the socket's input stream (its close order: each
pollable before its own parent, the socket's pollable last) traps:
"Cannot drop input stream as it has a child poll resource". The 11
corpus cases with `// skip-wasm-runner: jco` for this pass with it.
Against jco main 7a9b8c0c, packages/preview2-shim (0.31.1's
dist/io/worker-thread.bundle.js has the same lines).

diff --git a/packages/preview2-shim/src/io/worker-socket-tcp.ts b/packages/preview2-shim/src/io/worker-socket-tcp.ts
index 5b3d1f2..5ad39a5 100644
--- a/packages/preview2-shim/src/io/worker-socket-tcp.ts
+++ b/packages/preview2-shim/src/io/worker-socket-tcp.ts
@@ -299,7 +299,14 @@ export function socketTcpAccept(id: number) {
         keepAlive: false,
         keepAliveIdleTime: 0,
         pendingAccepts: [],
-        pollState: accept.pollState,
+        // The socket's own: accept.pollState is the input stream's, and
+        // a pollable of one must not hold up dropping the other.
+        pollState: {
+            ready: true,
+            listener: null,
+            polls: [],
+            parentStream: null,
+        },
     });
     return [tcpSocketCnt, inputStream, outputStream];
 }
